('car-diagnostics-cost-uk','How Much Does Car Diagnostics Cost in the UK?','Diagnostic cost depends on symptom complexity, test time, equipment and access. A code scan is not the same as finding the cause.','Advice & Guides','Car Diagnostics Cost in the UK | SOB Autofix','How much does car diagnostics cost in the UK? Learn what affects the quote, what proper fault finding includes and why a cheap code read may cost more later.', $body$
The cost of car diagnostics in the UK depends on the symptom, vehicle, equipment, access and time required to prove the fault. A basic code read can be quick, while an intermittent electrical or mechanical problem may require road testing, circuit measurements and monitored data over several hours. SOB Autofix does not publish a single invented price for every case: request an accurate quote based on the vehicle and symptoms. Ask what the quoted diagnostic stage includes and whether further testing requires authorisation.

## Code reading versus diagnosis

Connecting a scan tool retrieves diagnostic trouble codes and data reported by control modules. That is an important first step, but a code usually describes a detected condition, circuit or relationship. It rarely proves that the component named in a short description should be replaced.

Diagnosis interprets the code alongside symptoms, technical information and physical tests. An airflow code might require intake smoke testing and wiring measurements; a starting fault may need voltage-drop and fuel-pressure checks; a vibration may need mechanical inspection rather than scanning at all. Comparing prices without comparing scope can be misleading.

## What affects diagnostic cost?

- **Time and intermittency:** a permanent fault is often quicker to reproduce than one appearing only after rain or a long journey.
- **Vehicle access:** buried connectors or components require more preparation than accessible test points.
- **System complexity:** network, high-current and multiple-module faults can need specialised equipment and information.
- **The required test:** pressure, smoke, oscilloscope, current, mechanical or road tests involve different setup.
- **Previous intervention:** cleared codes, swapped parts and altered wiring can remove evidence or introduce additional faults.

The cheapest initial offer is not automatically the lowest final cost. Unproven parts replacement adds parts, labour and fresh uncertainty while the original complaint remains.

## What a diagnostic quote should explain

Ask whether the charge covers an initial investigation period, a specific test package or diagnosis through to a confirmed cause. A fair process may pause after initial findings and request approval for deeper dismantling or intermittent monitoring. This keeps control of spending without promising that every complex fault fits one fixed session.

Also ask what output you receive: stored codes, measurements, the evidence-based conclusion and recommended next step. Raw codes alone are not equivalent to a repair plan. If repair is authorised, clarify whether any diagnostic charge is separate; do not assume it is automatically included or waived.

## Why garages charge for diagnostic time

Professional diagnosis uses skilled labour, maintained tools, subscriptions, technical data and controlled test procedures. The value lies in reducing uncertainty. An hour spent proving a broken wire can save the cost of a control unit; a smoke test can separate a hose leak from a turbocharger fault.

Charging for investigation also supports an honest stopping point. When evidence does not yet justify a part, the technician can explain what is known and what additional test would answer the next question.

## Mobile diagnostics versus workshop testing

Some battery, starting, scan and electrical checks can be completed at the vehicle. A workshop is better when a lift, extensive dismantling, prolonged monitoring or specialist equipment is required. A mobile visit may avoid recovery but should not promise tests that the location cannot support safely.

SOB Autofix offers [vehicle diagnostics](/diagnostics/car-diagnostics) with mobile availability depending on the fault. The [mobile mechanic versus garage guide](/news/mobile-mechanic-vs-garage) helps you choose the suitable setting.

## Information that helps produce an accurate quote

Provide the registration, make and model, exact warning or symptom, when it occurs, whether the vehicle starts and drives safely, and any recent work. Photographs of messages and a short safe recording can help. Mention parts already replaced and whether codes have been cleared.

“Engine light on” may allow a defined initial scan and assessment. “Intermittently loses all dashboard communication after two hours in heavy rain” signals a different scope. Honest detail makes the estimate more useful and helps the technician bring appropriate equipment.

## Avoid false economy

Free or very cheap code reads can be useful information, but problems begin when a code is treated as a shopping list. Replacing a sensor will not repair a split hose, missing supply, corroded terminal or mechanical condition producing its reading. Ask what test proves the proposed part is faulty.

Likewise, avoid paying repeatedly for separate scans without sharing earlier reports. A coherent diagnostic history allows the next technician to build on evidence instead of starting again.

## When diagnosis is urgent

Cost comparison should not delay action for red oil-pressure or temperature warnings, poor braking, unstable steering, smoke, fuel smell or a flashing engine light. Stop safely and arrange recovery when continued driving risks people or major damage. The first quote should then include how the non-driveable vehicle will be assessed.

## Getting a price from SOB Autofix

Use [Get a Quote](/get-a-quote) and describe the vehicle and fault. SOB Autofix can outline the appropriate first diagnostic stage and any mobile or workshop requirements without publishing a figure that may not fit your case. No repair should be assumed merely because testing has started; findings support a separate, informed decision.
